# Secrets for Kamal deployment.
#
# Every value resolves ENV-first, then falls back to a local file. Both paths must
# work:
#   * Local deploy    — nothing is exported; the fallback reads .env / the key file.
#   * GitHub Actions  — .env and the key files are gitignored and therefore ABSENT
#                       from the checkout, so the value must come from ENV
#                       (the workflow maps GitHub secrets into the environment).
#
# Previously every line read only the local file with `2>/dev/null`, so a deploy
# from Actions silently shipped an EMPTY master key and empty registry/Postgres
# passwords. The `${VAR:-fallback}` runs inside $( ), which Kamal evaluates through
# a shell (Kamal::Secrets::Dotenv::InlineCommandSubstitution), so bash default
# expansion is available even though the file itself is dotenv-formatted.

# Docker Hub
KAMAL_REGISTRY_USERNAME=$(echo "${KAMAL_REGISTRY_USERNAME:-$(grep '^KAMAL_REGISTRY_USERNAME=' .env 2>/dev/null | cut -d= -f2-)}")
KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=$(echo "${KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD:-$(grep '^KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=' .env 2>/dev/null | cut -d= -f2-)}")

# Rails credentials key.
#
# NOTE: this app uses PER-ENVIRONMENT credentials. In production Rails reads
# config/credentials/production.yml.enc and looks for the key in
# config/credentials/production.key — NOT config/master.key, which does not exist
# in this repo at all. The env var name is still RAILS_MASTER_KEY for per-env
# credentials (railties-8.1.3/lib/rails/application.rb:516 defaults
# env_key: "RAILS_MASTER_KEY"), so shipping the production.key contents under that
# name is correct. The old line read config/master.key and therefore always
# resolved to an empty string, even on a local deploy.
RAILS_MASTER_KEY=$(echo "${RAILS_MASTER_KEY:-$(cat config/credentials/production.key 2>/dev/null || cat config/master.key 2>/dev/null)}")

# Self-hosted Postgres (production) — runs as a Kamal accessory on the app box.
# Production database.yml uses explicit host/port/user/password/database fields
# (not a URL) — the underscore in the Kamal accessory hostname (`learning_routes-postgres`)
# trips Ruby's URI registry-vs-server-authority discrimination in db:prepare.
# So we only ship POSTGRES_PASSWORD here; the rest is hardcoded in database.yml.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=$(echo "${POSTGRES_PASSWORD:-$(grep '^POSTGRES_PASSWORD=' .env 2>/dev/null | cut -d= -f2-)}")

# App third-party secrets (OpenAI, ElevenLabs, Tavily, Brevo SMTP, Google OAuth)
# are NO LONGER shipped as ENV. They live in encrypted Rails credentials and are
# decrypted at runtime via RAILS_MASTER_KEY above. Nothing to add here for them.
